WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.520
- Thanks to Curiosity
Stream for supporting PBS.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.057
- Golden Hour, Kacey Musgraves!

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.820
- Kacey Musgraves won Album of the Year

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.540
at the 2019 GRAMMYs for
her album, Golden Hour,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.620
becoming just the fourth country album

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.380
to ever take home that award.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.500
So how did she achieve
this crossover success,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.270
and what does it mean for
the future of country music?

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.750
We're going to talk to the producers of

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.293
Golden Hour to find out.

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:27.870
- The way that this album came together

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.350
with Kacey was just so natural between

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.410
the three of us, Daniel,
my co-producer, and Kacey.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.680
- I didn't have low expectations,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.880
like no one's gonna like this,

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.020
but I wasn't like, "we're
gonna slay the GRAMMYs".

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.380
I never thought that.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.600
- Over the past decade, country music,

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.810
the genre of Dolly Parton
and The Dixie Chicks

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.450
has been dominated by male perspectives.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.274
In 2015, a radio consultant suggested

00:00:49.274 --> 00:00:51.080
country stations shouldn't play

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.070
too many females if they
wanted solid ratings,

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.450
saying, "the lettuce is Luke
Bryan and Blake Shelton,

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.840
Keith Urban... the tomatoes of our salad

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.350
are the females."

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.317
The year before that,
Maddie and Tae released

00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:03.840
"Girl In The Country Song".

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.530
They're the mint on the state of females

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:06.980
in country music.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.980
("Girl In The Country
Song" by Maddie and Tae)

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.990
- So how does this genre evolve

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.720
and sound contemporary
while keeping its identity?

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.636
Some people argue that it's
crossing over into Pop.

00:01:19.636 --> 00:01:23.440
("Women" by Georgia Florida Line)

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.935
And others say it means
doubling down on tradition.

00:01:25.935 --> 00:01:29.480
("I Don't Remember Me"
by Brothers Osborne)

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.618
Kacey Musgraves shows that this choice is

00:01:31.618 --> 00:01:33.688
a false binary by mixing elements of

00:01:33.688 --> 00:01:36.990
the past and the future
throughout Golden Hour.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.690
- In a way, I would argue that a lot of

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:43.280
mainstream country is even
more overtly pop oriented.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.440
In mainstream country music, the kind of

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.070
country music that's played on the radio,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.270
you hear much more overtly Pop elements

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.800
like drum machines and trap.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.900
- Throughout the album,
Kacey Musgraves takes

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.720
old traditional sounds and makes them

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.965
sound new by recontextualizing them.

00:01:58.965 --> 00:02:01.310
- You know, I think Kacey
had a real clear vision

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:03.320
that she wanted to mix those things that

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.630
she had on her other albums with some

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:08.180
new sounds to bring those worlds together.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.300
- She previously hadn't
had a lot of, like,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.530
keyboards on her records, and so it's

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:14.650
interesting even just
introducing the piano

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.360
and some of the synthesizers,
and she was really

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.500
open-minded and free
to explore that stuff.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.040
- Even one of the most
futuristic sounding moments

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:23.660
in that album has roots in the past.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.140
Check out the robotic
vocoder at the beginning of

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.771
Kacey Musgraves' song, "Oh, What A World".

00:02:28.771 --> 00:02:33.080
("Oh, What A World" by Kacey Musgraves)

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:34.810
- Now check out country legend Pete Drake

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.877
in the 1965 country music film,

00:02:36.877 --> 00:02:39.089
"Second Fiddle To A Steel Guitar".

00:02:39.089 --> 00:02:43.927
("Second Fiddle To A Steel
Guitar" by Pete Drake)

00:02:43.927 --> 00:02:45.320
- The vocoder at the beginning.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.630
- That's actually my voice,
which is really weird

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.090
to hear now, like, when you hear it at

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.050
a live concert or
something. You know, it's

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:53.668
not unusual for me to
hear, like, my drums or

00:02:53.668 --> 00:02:56.170
piano or guitar parts that
I might have recorded,

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.710
but it's definitely a
different feeling to hear

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.660
your voice through a vocoder.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.350
- What was that a reference to?

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:03.890
- I think Kacey just had that idea,

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:05.960
she might've referred
specifically to Daft Punk,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.490
but I don't know, I think she just kind of

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.409
identified the vocoder's sound as

00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:11.910
something that could sound extra-worldly.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.480
- So while this kind of
effect suggests futurism,

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.200
it's also distinctly retro.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.580
In fact, here's what
Kacey Musgraves told GQ

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.960
about "Oh, What A World".

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.220
It's this trippy
perspective, this sound where

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:23.980
future meets tradition.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.240
One though I had was
what if Imogen Heap made

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.270
a country album? Would it work?

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.020
Would it make sense?

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.380
- Maybe no instrument or
sound is more associated

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.680
with country music than
Pedal Steel Guitar.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.600
- In fact, when George
Harrison and Bob Dylan

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.850
wanted to make country albums,

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.240
they had help from Pedal
Steel player, Pete Drake,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.343
the talk box guy.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.610
However, on Golden Hour,
the pedal steel guitar is

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:48.730
doing something different.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.100
It's used more like a texture,

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.720
similar to how a post-rock band

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:52.790
might use a guitar.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:54.870
Check out the guitar
in this post-rock song

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.213
by Sigur Ros.

00:03:59.947 --> 00:04:02.430
And here's how Kacey uses
the pedal steel sound

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.567
in "Space Cowboy".

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:08.310
("Space Cowboy" by Kacey Musgraves)

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.730
- Some bands have experimented with

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:11.010
the texture of their instruments to

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.400
create post-rock.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.520
Is Kacey doing a similar
thing with country to

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.090
make post-country?

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:20.030
- Country music is a big
part of her story, her life,

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.740
and in her heart, she
really loves country music.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.670
We weren't making rules saying we have to

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.090
make sure that this appeals to country,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.580
especially because she's never really

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:30.740
been embraced by country radio.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:32.151
- That's something you guys think about

00:04:32.151 --> 00:04:35.402
on a regular basis? The identity of

00:04:35.402 --> 00:04:37.540
where you guys are coming from?

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.220
- I think it was more of like, her saying

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.670
I really do love the
emotion of the pedal steel.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.330
- I think Kacey was
like, look, we're gonna

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:45.460
turn the banjo up, we're
gonna turn the steel up,

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:46.850
so we're gonna let people know those

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:48.700
things are in there, and I think she kinda

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.220
wanted to do that because
if you're not mixing

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.270
genres, in a way, it's
probably not that interesting.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.770
- Throughout the process,
Kacey made sure that

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.993
the experimentation didn't go too far.

00:04:57.993 --> 00:05:00.440
- One thing that we always
kinda had joked about

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.750
was that Kacey's just really good.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.730
We called her The Axeman because she was

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:05.850
always good at trimming the fat.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.130
- And I think The Axeman
ended up really doing

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.554
us a great service because The Axeman gave

00:05:11.554 --> 00:05:15.290
us a lot of space in music
in a way that I think

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.720
allows people to really lean into it, and

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.017
listen to it as opposed to being like,

00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:21.481
"whoa, there's all this
music coming at me".

00:05:21.481 --> 00:05:22.890
(laughs)

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:26.240
- So Shawn Everett is
credited with Dolphin Magic.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.590
Can you tell us what Dolphin Magic is?

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:30.500
- I don't know if it's
ever been made public,

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.000
on this song, "Happy &amp;
Sad", there's a transition

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.160
that goes into the chorus that is this

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:39.070
celestial kind of heart sound.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:41.400
It's lifted from a particular cartoon,

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.050
I think there's an image of a dolphin

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.630
jumping out of the water
as the sound occurs,

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:46.830
and we just pulled that.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.690
- There may be another
factor that's playing into

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:52.630
the crossover success of Golden Hour.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:54.390
The rise of the Yeehaw Agenda,

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.750
starting on the internet as memes in 2017,

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.360
to finding it's viral moment with

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.104
yodeling kid, Mason Ramsey in 2018.

00:06:01.104 --> 00:06:03.354
(yodeling)

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:08.150
From both Solange and Cardi B wearing

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:10.810
cowgirl outfits to Lil Nas X's Billboard

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:12.692
number one, Old Town Road, being a cowboy

00:06:12.692 --> 00:06:14.810
has suddenly become very cool.

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.620
This rise of the Yeehaw
Agenda has also opened

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.530
the doors for people of
all different backgrounds

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.160
to participate in the aesthetic of

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:22.490
country Western music.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:24.003
When a fan tweeted this,

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.663
Kacey responded with this.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.560
- Kacey's lyrics open
the door even further,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.227
the inclusive messages of
songs like her 2013 hit,

00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:37.330
"Follow Your Arrow" makes
her a perfect ambassador

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:39.623
for country music to a wider audience.

00:06:39.623 --> 00:06:44.373
("Follow Your Arrow" by Kacey Musgraves)

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.260
So the sudden love for cowboy style

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.070
may have helped her cross over to success,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.070
or she could be part of the reason Yeehaw

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.060
became so popular in the first place.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.830
There aren't many artists
who have performed

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.694
at the Houston Rodeo and appeared on

00:06:57.694 --> 00:06:59.510
Rupaul's Drag Race.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:03.200
Ellie and I are going to write
a Golden Hour inspired song.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.640
Do you have any tips for us?

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.220
- Overall, it's a bit reflective,

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.170
and so, you know, just
reflect on your life,

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:10.950
and get out of your
comfort zone a little bit,

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.631
and see what's out there.
Acoustic instruments

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:15.900
like the acoustic guitar
is pretty much everywhere.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.490
There's not a lot of cymbals!

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:21.710
- Our job is to do Kacey
Musgraves' style, country.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:25.960
- I listened to the
album, and made notes on

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:27.050
most of the tracks.

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:30.250
Usually the progressions have four chords,

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.240
or five, sometimes just two.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.240
One, six, three, five,
or one, six, three, four,

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:39.543
I wrote.

00:07:39.543 --> 00:07:42.540
- She puts a lot of
emphasis on the guitar sound

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.040
kinda thing, country music
does that in general,

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.453
has a lot of guitar sound, but how we..

00:07:48.453 --> 00:07:51.210
- How are we gonna, um, so I have an idea.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:52.043
- Okay.

00:07:52.043 --> 00:07:55.870
- So I think we could
try, at least, pulling off

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:59.308
putting in a guitar. I
own an acoustic guitar.

00:07:59.308 --> 00:08:00.250
- Okay.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:03.070
- I can kinda learn basic
chords, and just strum.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.670
- You have a guitar, and
you're just gonna teach

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:09.160
yourself, you're gonna
strum some chords on

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:10.050
this guitar?

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.743
- Why not? Hold on, I have.. hold on.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.790
- You're just gonna teach yourself guitar.

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:20.370
- Yeah, if you just,
okay, so if you just play

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.569
just one chord and just
strum it here and there.

00:08:26.569 --> 00:08:27.760
(guitar strums)

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.113
This is not a good idea right now.

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:31.150
- Country, here we come.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.540
- Before we share what we came up with,

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:34.390
we'd like to thank Curiosity Stream for

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:36.895
supporting PBS. Curiosity Stream is a

00:08:36.895 --> 00:08:39.920
streaming service that
offers documentaries

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.340
and non-fiction titles from a variety of

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.320
filmmakers, including
Curiosity Stream originals.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.040
For example, they have
Unraveling The Creative Mind,

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:49.720
that's about how our world has been shaped

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.970
by those who have dared
to think differently.

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.643
You can learn more at
curiositystream.com/soundfield.

00:08:55.819 --> 00:08:59.493
- Okay, now here's our
Golden Hour inspired track.

00:09:00.822 --> 00:09:03.989
(country music plays)

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.790
- We need some help with the vocals, so

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:32.680
download our track from our Soundcloud

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.070
at the link in the description,

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:34.903
and sing on it.

00:09:34.903 --> 00:09:36.750
And here's a little advice
from Daniel Tashian.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.560
- Country music likes
a lot of furniture in

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.800
the song, where other
songs talk about feelings

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.113
like, country music
likes a lot of objects.

